  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Between June 10 and June 17, US and Iranian officials concluded back-channel negotiations through Pakistan, Qatar, and Oman that produced the 14-point "Islamabad Memorandum": a 60-day ceasefire framework signed by Trump at Versailles and by President Pezeshkian in Tehran on June 17. The MOU established a Hormuz transit mechanism, waived sanctions on Iranian oil exports and petrochemicals, released a tranche of frozen Iranian assets, and obtained an Iranian commitment to allow IAEA inspector access. The first formal face-to-face session between US and Iranian delegations convened at Lake Lucerne (June 21-22), co-mediated by Pakistan and Qatar, with the parties agreeing on a "roadmap to a final deal" and a Lebanon deconfliction cell. A second round in Doha (June 30–July 1), also Qatar-facilitated, established a bilateral communications channel for recording MOU violations and registered Iran's demand for a $6 billion frozen-asset release as a precondition for further progress. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
      <p>The June 7-8 Israeli-Iranian exchange — in which the IDF struck sites in Tehran, Isfahan, and Tabriz, and Iran fired approximately 30 ballistic missiles at three Israeli air bases — ended when both sides stopped operations on June 8 after a Trump-requested pause. Within the F3 resolution window (June 10-24), new ceasefire terms took effect on June 12, and the Islamabad MOU signed June 17 formally ended open bilateral Israel-Iran exchanges for the duration of the 60-day negotiating period. No confirmed bilateral military exchange (strikes in both directions within any 48-hour window) between Israel and Iran occurred between June 10 and June 24. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
      <p>CENTCOM announced a new phase of coordinated MCM (mine countermeasure) operations on June 16 under the MOU framework, deploying US LCS vessels, Avenger-class minesweepers, autonomous UUVs, and allied forces from France, the UK, Germany, Italy, Japan, and Canada. Commercial transits through the Strait's southern corridor recovered: JMIC recorded a single-day count of 54 transits on June 24, and Lloyd's List Intelligence counted 258 transits in the week ending June 28 (approximately 37 per day on a 7-day average). Iranian strikes on commercial vessels on June 25 and June 27 disrupted the recovery, and the 7-day rolling average did not reach 50 before the July 2 deadline; Signal Ocean's AIS-based 7-day average stood at approximately 8 traceable transits on July 1. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
      <p>Israel's Knesset dissolution bill passed its first reading 106-0 on June 2 and remained in committee between June 10 and July 10. The Knesset Legal Adviser formally recommended dissolution on July 17 — two days after the F6 deadline — and both Knesset Speaker Ohana and Coalition Whip Katz publicly confirmed the July 17 timetable. Netanyahu's coalition bloc held to October 27 as the preferred election date (statutory maximum), resisting UTJ and Shas pressure for a September date. No second or third readings occurred before July 10, placing the dissolution trajectory outside the July 15 window required for F6 resolution, with the expected election date (October 27) also failing to satisfy the "earlier than 2026-10-27" criterion. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
      <p>The Ali Khamenei state funeral, held July 3-9 with representatives from more than 100 nations, concluded on July 9 in Mashhad. On July 6, IRGC forces fired missiles at three commercial vessels in Omani territorial waters; on July 7, the United States struck more than 80 Iranian targets and reimposed oil export sanctions; on July 8, Trump declared the Islamabad MOU ceasefire "over" and US forces struck approximately 90 additional Iranian targets along Iran's coastline. Iran launched retaliatory missiles and drones at military facilities in Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, Jordan, and Iraq. By July 9-10, Lloyd's List reported no large AIS-broadcasting vessel had crossed Hormuz's southern route since July 7, with approximately 6,000 seafarers stranded on vessels in the Gulf. Pakistan and Qatar continued back-channel mediation. New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei had still not made any verified public appearance as of July 10 — four months after his appointment on March 9. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:30%;">Escalation 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:45%;">Protracted 45%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:25%;">De-esc 25%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Iran expands strikes beyond initial Gulf retaliations toward broader Gulf infrastructure or Israeli territory while US continues airstrikes without a ceasefire framework, risking direct IRGC-US naval confrontation in or near Hormuz; blocked by mediator pressure from Pakistan/Qatar and US domestic political cost of sustained open-ended war.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Intermittent exchanges continue below the threshold of full war, with Hormuz partially reopening and closing episodically and diplomacy stalling until one side's strategic calculus shifts; the 21-day N4 horizon (formal talks by July 31, p=35%) is the primary exit signal from this scenario.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Pakistan/Qatar mediation yields a narrow renewed ceasefire within N1's 3-day window (p=30%) or formal talks reconvene under N4 (p=35% by July 31), stopping active exchanges and allowing MCM operations to resume toward partial Hormuz recovery within N5's August 31 window.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Press TV, Tasnim) framed the Islamabad MOU during June as a diplomatic and economic victory for the Islamic Republic: sanctions waivers, partial frozen-asset releases, and a US-backed reconstruction commitment were presented as proof that American-Israeli aggression had failed to bring Iran to its knees. The June-era media narrative emphasized continuity of governance and the resilience of the "resistance economy." Pezeshkian's government received favorable coverage for obtaining these concessions while continuing to refer to the MOU as a step toward permanent recognition of Iranian sovereignty. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The governance vacuum around Mojtaba Khamenei — the new Supreme Leader has released only written statements attributed to him by state media since his appointment on March 9 — produced two parallel narratives. Official channels maintained that he was in "complete health" with minor injuries; simultaneously, foreign outlets (NYT, Reuters) cited Iranian officials describing coma, potential leg amputation, and severe facial burns. Mojtaba's absence during the Ali Khamenei state funeral (July 3-9), the highest-profile collective mourning event since the war began, received no acknowledgment in state media, which instead focused on mass attendance figures and foreign dignitaries. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>After the IRGC's July 6 strikes on Hormuz commercial ships and Trump's July 8 "ceasefire over" declaration, state media reverted to full wartime resistance framing. IRGC attacks on Gulf states (Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait) were presented as retaliatory defense against American aggression, not as violations of the Islamabad MOU. The civilian-security establishment gap — Pezeshkian signed the MOU while IRGC continued independent operations — remained unacknowledged in official communications, with state media treating IRGC and government actions as a unified Iranian response to unprovoked American bombardment. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

